# Line endings for the decision log and the frozen artifacts.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on this machine, so by default git checks text files
# out with CRLF while storing them with LF. That is harmless for code, but it
# would silently break the append-only proof: scripts/verify_decisions_split.py
# reassembles decisions/* and diffs the result against the LF blob of the old
# DECISIONS.md that git still holds. On a fresh clone the working files would be
# CRLF, the blob LF, and every single line would report as changed. The proof
# would look like evidence of tampering when nothing had been touched.
#
# eol=lf pins these files to LF in the working tree on every platform, so the
# comparison stays byte-for-byte meaningful.
STATE.md            text eol=lf
decisions/**.md     text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FROZEN ARTIFACTS (added 2026-07-30)
#
# Measured before this block was written, with `git ls-files --eol`:
# 55 files under reports/ were stored LF in the blob but sat CRLF in the working
# tree, both canonical states among them. Unpinned, their bytes depend on the
# checkout, so "this artifact is frozen" was true of its CONTENT but not of its
# BYTES, and any future byte-level comparison across two machines would differ
# for a reason that has nothing to do with the measurement.
#
# WHY `text eol=lf` HERE AND NOT `-text`, which is the stronger-sounding pin.
# `-text` means "never convert", so it would freeze the CURRENT CRLF working
# copies and, on the next renormalize or fresh checkout, rewrite all 55 frozen
# artifacts' blobs from LF to CRLF. Identical content, different blob hash, on
# files whose whole purpose is to never change. `text eol=lf` reaches the same
# end state (byte-identical on every platform, no false positive on a fresh
# clone) while leaving every existing blob exactly as committed. The goal is
# byte-stability, and here the gentler attribute is the one that delivers it.
#
# The .npz index arrays need no such argument: git already detects them as
# binary (`i/-text w/-text`), so `-text` below is a written-down statement of
# what is already true, and costs nothing.
reports/**/*.json   text eol=lf
reports/**/*.md     text eol=lf
reports/**/*.npz    -text
reports/**/*.png    -text
